<h2>1. Project Description</h2>
<p>In modern corporate enterprises such as <strong>NexGen Technologies Pvt. Ltd.</strong> (a mid-sized IT consulting and software engineering firm with 500 employees located in Sector 62, Noida), financial operations handle thousands of monetary transactions every month across multiple commercial banking partners (e.g., HDFC Bank, ICICI Bank, State Bank of India). These financial workflows are fundamentally divided into two major transaction mechanisms:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Debit Transactions:</strong> Direct outlays from corporate operating checking accounts representing immediate cash deductions (e.g., monthly payroll disbursements, cloud infrastructure invoices, office lease, statutory tax payments, and routine supplier fees). Funds leave liquid reserves instantly.</li>
    <li><strong>Credit Transactions:</strong> Expenditures executed via corporate revolving credit cards and short-term credit facilities (e.g., employee business travel and lodging, client entertainment, SaaS software licenses, emergency hardware repairs, and digital advertising). These represent short-term debt obligations that accumulate over a monthly billing cycle and must be paid before due dates to prevent high interest penalties (up to 42% APR) and cash advance surcharges.</li>
</ul>
<p>The <strong>Credit vs Debit Transaction Analysis</strong> application is an interactive decision-support and business intelligence platform built using <strong>Python, Streamlit, SQLite, and Plotly</strong>. It centralizes disparate transaction streams, automatically categorizes debit vs. credit spending patterns, tracks corporate liquidity burn and credit line utilization ratios, pinpoints temporal and merchant spending concentrations, detects statistical expense anomalies, and surfaces actionable insights to executive finance managers through high-impact analytical dashboards.</p>

<h2>2. Background: Current State & Problem Context</h2>
<p>Currently at NexGen Technologies, financial records from multiple corporate credit card accounts and liquid checking accounts arrive at month-end in fragmented CSV, Excel, and scanned PDF formats. The 4-member finance operations desk relies heavily on manual Microsoft Excel workbooks, VLOOKUP formulas, and pivot tables to reconcile expenses.</p>
<p>This manual operating model exhibits severe systemic drawbacks:</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Extreme Reporting Lag:</strong> Full reconciliation and management reporting takes between 7 to 10 business days after the close of the calendar month. During this lag, executive management operates blind to real-time liquidity changes.</li>
    <li><strong>High Discrepancy & Error Rates:</strong> Manual cut-and-paste consolidation and unstandardized merchant descriptions introduce an estimated 10% to 15% error margin, including misclassified expense heads and undetected duplicate payments.</li>
    <li><strong>Uncontrolled Credit Utilization:</strong> Because credit card spending is inspected retroactively, corporate credit utilization regularly breaches the recommended 70% threshold, exposing the company to steep penalty APRs and unfavorable banking terms.</li>
    <li><strong>Delayed Anomaly & Leakage Detection:</strong> Duplicate vendor charges, off-policy employee card charges, or unusual spending surges are caught weeks later during statutory accounting reviews rather than when they occur.</li>
    <li><strong>Siloed Visibility:</strong> Departmental heads (IT, HR, Marketing) have zero self-service visibility into their team's monthly budget consumption across liquid debit versus revolving credit.</li>
</ol>

<h2>3. Problem Statement</h2>
<div class='problem-box'>
"The finance operations team at NexGen Technologies experiences an unsustainable 7-to-10-day reporting latency and recurring reconciliation errors due to fragmented, manual spreadsheet processing of credit and debit banking data, preventing leadership from proactively monitoring cash burn, optimizing revolving credit utilization, and detecting anomalous financial leaks."
</div>

<h2>4. Intended Audience & User Personas</h2>
<table>
    <tr><th>User Persona</th><th>Role in Organization</th><th>Access Frequency</th><th>Primary Usage of the Dashboard</th></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Accounts Executive</strong></td><td>Junior Finance Desk Operator</td><td>Daily / Twice Weekly</td><td>Uploads transaction batches, monitors daily debit cash outflows, verifies credit charge postings, and tags anomalous or unclassified vendor expenses.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Finance Manager / Controller</strong></td><td>Operational Finance Head</td><td>Weekly / Monthly Close</td><td>Reviews weekly burn rates, monitors credit line utilization vs. bank debit reserves, approves supplier settlements, and ensures timely credit card bill clearance.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Chief Financial Officer (CFO)</strong></td><td>Executive Leadership</td><td>Monthly / Quarterly</td><td>Analyzes high-level cash flow trends, evaluates credit vs debit expenditure proportions, forecasts quarterly working capital needs, and renegotiates banking limits.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Internal Auditor / Risk Lead</strong></td><td>Compliance & Risk Governance</td><td>Monthly / Audit Cycles</td><td>Scrutinizes flagged statistical anomalies (>3 sigma), verifies adherence to corporate spending policies, and cross-checks payment channel failure rates.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Department Heads (IT / HR / Ops)</strong></td><td>Cost Center Owners</td><td>Monthly</td><td>Tracks departmental budget utilization across software subscriptions, travel, and operational procurement.</td></tr>
</table>

